  "summary": "Oval frame drums painted with the whole Sámi cosmos — used to read the future.",
  "description": "The Sámi noaidi's ceremonial drum (goavddis) is a shallow oval frame of bent pine over which reindeer hide is stretched and painted with red alder-bark figures: the sun (Beaivi) at center, gods and sieidi spirits, reindeer herds, hunting scenes, and the layered worlds of Sámi cosmology mapped as if the drumhead were a chart of existence. Striking it with a T-shaped horn hammer while reading the movements of a brass ring across the figures gave answers. Seventeenth-century missionaries burned or confiscated hundreds of drums as witchcraft — under 100 survive in museums — and their painted cosmograms remain the densest record of pre-Christian Sámi worldview, now echoed in art and political symbolism.",
  "culturalContext": "The noaidi's drum — confiscated as witchcraft in the 1600s-1700s — became Sámi cultural memory's densest document: under 100 survive in European museums, their painted cosmograms feed 20th-century revivals and political symbolism (the drum on Sámi flags of the mind), and repatriation debates make it central to Sámi heritage claims.",
